1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,301
An AI agent can propose infrastructure changes in seconds.

2
00:00:04,301 --> 00:00:08,269
But who decides whether those changes are actually allowed?

3
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:13,405
Every node in a cloud is a resource someone depends on.

4
00:00:13,405 --> 00:00:17,994
Here, an agent was asked to raise one Lambda function's memory.

5
00:00:17,994 --> 00:00:24,112
Its edit also loosened an S3 bucket's public access block, and added a network rule.

6
00:00:24,112 --> 00:00:25,714
Nobody asked for that.

7
00:00:25,714 --> 00:00:27,900
And nobody had drawn the line.

8
00:00:29,399 --> 00:00:32,080
PlanReview makes that line explicit.

9
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:34,611
A human confirms a boundary first.

10
00:00:34,611 --> 00:00:40,270
Then every proposed change is judged against it, before anything is applied.

11
00:00:41,399 --> 00:00:49,239
It starts with a contract: which resources may change, which operations, how many, and what is forbidden.

12
00:00:49,239 --> 00:00:53,794
An agent edits Terraform, and Terraform produces a real plan.

13
00:00:53,794 --> 00:00:57,378
Cedar policies then give every change a verdict.

14
00:00:57,378 --> 00:01:00,364
Allow, when it sits inside the boundary.

15
00:01:00,364 --> 00:01:02,828
Review, when a human must decide.

16
00:01:02,828 --> 00:01:06,039
And deny, when it crosses a forbidden line.

17
00:01:06,039 --> 00:01:10,220
Every verdict shows its reason and the policy behind it.

18
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:12,758
A deny can never be approved away.

19
00:01:12,758 --> 00:01:22,539
The apply gate opens only when nothing is denied, and every step is written to a tamper-evident audit chain that anyone can verify.

20
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:29,352
Under the hood, a FastAPI backend drives the pipeline.

21
00:01:29,352 --> 00:01:31,728
Terraform creates the plan.

22
00:01:31,728 --> 00:01:35,337
A canonicalizer normalizes and hashes it.

23
00:01:35,337 --> 00:01:36,569
Cedar decides.

24
00:01:36,569 --> 00:01:40,529
SQLite stores tasks and an H-MAC audit chain.

25
00:01:40,529 --> 00:01:44,930
The front end is plain JavaScript, with three J S.

26
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:48,183
How does it use AWS?

27
00:01:48,183 --> 00:01:56,813
Plans are written for the AWS Terraform provider, covering Lambda functions, S3 buckets, and security groups.

28
00:01:56,813 --> 00:02:01,009
Permitted changes apply only to a local AWS emulator.

29
00:02:01,009 --> 00:02:08,926
Cedar is the language behind Amazon Verified Permissions, but PlanReview does not call that service.

30
00:02:08,926 --> 00:02:13,360
No real AWS account is touched, and nothing is deployed.

31
00:02:14,599 --> 00:02:16,451
Back to that same change.

32
00:02:16,451 --> 00:02:20,672
PlanReview does not let the public access change through.

33
00:02:20,672 --> 00:02:23,856
The boundary stays where the human drew it.

34
00:02:23,856 --> 00:02:24,671
PlanReview.

35
00:02:24,671 --> 00:02:28,299
Human intent, explicit infrastructure boundaries.

